1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:06,050
Chapter 31: Prior Cesarean Delivery.

3
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:27,900
100 Years Of Controversy Influencing Factors Delivery Route Risks Trial Of Labor Selection Considerations Labor And Delivery Considerations Uterine Scar Rupture Multiple Repeat Cesarean Deliveries Vaginal Birth After Cesarean—2021 References By the beginning of the 20th century, cesarean delivery had become a relatively safe procedure.

4
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:33,437
However, rupture of the uterine scar with subsequent labor was appreciated early.

5
00:00:33,437 --> 00:00:41,437
This danger resulted in the oftquoted remark by Cragin (1916) that “Once a cesarean, always a cesarean.

6
00:00:41,437 --> 00:00:48,662
During the intervening 50 years, enthusiasm developed to allow many of these women to be delivered vaginally.

7
00:00:48,662 --> 00:00:54,075
But, as chronicled next, this support has waned during the past three decades.

8
00:00:54,075 --> 00:01:01,150
So, as we reach the 100-year mark of Cragin’s pronouncement, the issue remains largely unsettled.

9
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:03,937
100 Years Of Controversy.

10
00:01:03,937 --> 00:01:12,062
Despite Cragin’s early philosophy, some practitioners did allow labor in a subsequent pregnancy after a cesarean delivery.

11
00:01:12,062 --> 00:01:19,850
Eastman (1950) described a 30-percent postcesarean vaginal delivery rate at Johns Hopkins Hospital.

12
00:01:19,850 --> 00:01:26,450
He also reported a 2-percent uterine rupture rate and associated 10-percent maternal mortality rate.

13
00:01:26,450 --> 00:01:41,000
As modern techniques made cesarean delivery safer, observational studies during the 1960s suggested that postcesarean vaginal delivery was a reasonable option (Pauerstein, 1966, 1969).

14
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:48,612
Germane to this is that through the 1960s, the overall cesarean delivery rate approximated only 5 percent.

15
00:01:48,612 --> 00:01:55,487
Since then, as the primary cesarean rate has escalated, the repeat cesarean delivery rate followed .

16
00:01:55,487 --> 00:02:10,887
During the 1980s, a National Institutes of Health (NIH) Consensus Development Conference on Vaginal Birth After Cesarean (1981) was convened, and the participants questioned the necessity of routine repeat cesarean delivery.

17
00:02:10,887 --> 00:02:25,687
With support and encouragement from the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (1988, 1994), enthusiastic attempts were begun to increase the practice rate of vaginal birth after cesarean—VBAC.

18
00:02:25,687 --> 00:02:36,362
These attempts were highly successful, and VBAC rates rose from 3.4 percent in 1980 to a peak of 28.3 percent in 1996.

19
00:02:36,362 --> 00:02:44,550
This rate and a concomitant decline in total cesarean delivery rate for the United States, are shown in Figure 31-1.

20
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:50,000
Also, a new lexicon was developed to describe these practices and outcomes .

21
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,575
Figure 31-1.

22
00:02:52,575 --> 00:03:05,412
Total, primary, and low-risk cesarean delivery (CD) rates and vaginal birth after previous cesarean (VBAC) rates in the United States, 1989–2018.

23
00:03:05,412 --> 00:03:11,962
Epochs denoted within rectangles represent contemporaneous ongoing events related to these rates.

24
00:03:11,962 --> 00:03:21,812
ACOG = American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists; NIH = National Institutes of Health; PB = practice bulletin.

25
00:03:21,812 --> 00:03:33,800
Data from Hamilton, 2015, 2016; Martin, 2019; National Institutes of Health: NIH Consensus Development Conference, 2010.

26
00:03:33,800 --> 00:03:36,362
Table 31-1.

27
00:03:36,362 --> 00:03:54,925
Acronyms Used to Describe Management of Women with a Prior Cesarean Delivery However, with the higher postcesarean vaginal delivery rate, reports of uterine rupture-related maternal and perinatal morbidity and mortality began to appear (McMahon, 1996; Sachs, 1999).

28
00:03:54,925 --> 00:04:02,187
These complications dampened prevailing enthusiasm for a trial of labor after cesarean section (TOLAC).

29
00:04:02,187 --> 00:04:17,000
They also prompted the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (1998) to caution that such trials should be attempted only in appropriately equipped institutions with physicians readily available to provide emergency care.

30
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:25,662
Less than a year later, the College (1999) recommended that physicians should be immediately available when pursuing a TOLAC.

31
00:04:25,662 --> 00:04:42,012
Many believe that this one-word change—from readily to immediately available—was an impetus for the resulting decade-long decline in national VBAC rates illustrated in Figure 31-1 (Cheng, 2014; Leeman, 2013).

32
00:04:42,012 --> 00:04:56,112
In a cross-sectional analysis between 1990 and 2009, the proportion of women with a prior cesarean delivery who underwent a TOLAC peaked in 1995, and slightly more than half of these women chose this option .

33
00:04:56,112 --> 00:05:00,150
After this, the proportion of women attempting TOLAC

34
00:05:00,316 --> 00:05:08,116
declined to a nadir of 16 percent in 2006 and subsequently rose to 21 percent through 2009.

35
00:05:08,066 --> 00:05:17,991
Additionally, the percentage of VBACs reached its peak of 70 percent in 2000 but subsequently declined to a nadir of 38 percent in 2006 .

36
00:05:17,991 --> 00:05:20,578
Figure 31-2.

37
00:05:20,578 --> 00:05:33,728
Percentage of women with a prior cesarean delivery who attempted a trial of labor (TOLAC) compared with the successful vaginal delivery (VBAC) rate for the United States 1990– 2009.

38
00:05:33,728 --> 00:05:45,603
Data from Centers for Disease Control and Prevention; National Center for Health Statistics; Curtin, 2015; Simon, 2013; Uddin, 2013).

39
00:05:45,603 --> 00:05:57,516
Using birth certificate data for 2013, Curtin and associates (2015) reported that only 1 in 5 women with one prior cesarean delivery attempted a TOLAC.

40
00:05:57,516 --> 00:06:07,078
In women with two or more prior cesarean deliveries, less than 1 in 10 women attempt a TOLAC, and half resulted in successful vaginal deliveries.

41
00:06:07,078 --> 00:06:14,941
At Parkland Hospital, although only 15 percent of women choose a TOLAC, the VBAC rate is 86 percent.

42
00:06:14,941 --> 00:06:23,266
Thus, multiple interrelated factors—both medical and nonmedical— undoubtedly contributed to declining VBAC rates.

43
00:06:23,266 --> 00:06:30,528
Because of this, in 2010 the NIH convened a Consensus Development Conference Panel to study the issue.

44
00:06:30,528 --> 00:06:38,578
The panel report included a contemporaneous summary concerning the risks and benefits of repeat cesarean versus vaginal delivery.

45
00:06:38,578 --> 00:06:46,128
These findings were subsequently described and coupled with current recommendations by various professional organizations.

46
00:06:46,128 --> 00:06:53,503
As shown in Figure 31-1, it would seem that this report was followed by a slight rise in the VBAC rate.

47
00:06:53,503 --> 00:06:55,828
Influencing Factors.

48
00:06:55,828 --> 00:07:06,941
For the woman with a prior cesarean delivery, planning for future pregnancies and delivery route should begin with preconceptional counseling and be addressed again early in prenatal care.

49
00:07:06,941 --> 00:07:17,116
Importantly, decisions made throughout pregnancy regarding delivery mode are subject to continuing revisions as dictated by exigencies that arise during pregnancy.

50
00:07:17,116 --> 00:07:22,066
Assuming no mitigating circumstances, there are two basic choices.

51
00:07:22,066 --> 00:07:26,516
First, a TOLAC offers the goal of achieving a VBAC.

52
00:07:26,516 --> 00:07:32,166
If cesarean delivery becomes necessary during the trial, it is a failed TOLAC.

53
00:07:32,166 --> 00:07:37,178
A second choice is elective repeat cesarean delivery (ERCD).

54
00:07:37,178 --> 00:07:46,466
This includes scheduled cesarean delivery as well as unscheduled but planned cesarean delivery for spontaneous labor or another delivery indication.

55
00:07:46,466 --> 00:07:54,478
The decision regarding delivery mode should weigh clinical factors known to influence TOLAC success as well as benefits and risks .

56
00:07:54,478 --> 00:07:59,778
As expected, TOLAC rates vary between institutions and providers.

57
00:07:59,778 --> 00:08:06,778
Last, economics, staffing, and medicolegal factors also may shape the decision to offer TOLAC.

58
00:08:06,778 --> 00:08:09,366
Table 31-2.

59
00:08:09,366 --> 00:08:18,066
Some Factors That Influence a Successful Trial of Labor in a Woman with Prior Cesarean Delivery (CD) Delivery Route Risks.

60
00:08:18,066 --> 00:08:39,466
As evidence mounted that the uterine rupture risk might be greater than expected, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (1988, 1994, 1998, 1999, 2015, 2019a,d) issued updated bulletins supporting TOLAC but also urging a more cautious approach.

61
00:08:39,466 --> 00:08:46,703
It is problematic that both options have risks and benefits to mother and fetus but that these are not always congruent.

62
00:08:46,703 --> 00:08:49,216
# Maternal Risks.

63
00:08:49,216 --> 00:08:55,041
Rates of uterine rupture and associated complications clearly are increased with TOLAC.

64
00:08:55,041 --> 00:09:10,528
As shown in Table 31-1, uterine rupture typically is classified as either: (1) complete, when all uterine wall layers are separated, or (2) incomplete, when the uterine muscle is separated but the visceral peritoneum is intact.

65
00:09:10,528 --> 00:09:15,403
Incomplete rupture is also commonly referred to as uterine dehiscence .

66
00:09:15,403 --> 00:09:20,041
It is these risks that underpin most of the angst in attempting TOLAC.

67
00:09:20,041 --> 00:09:27,328
Despite this, some argue that these factors should weigh only minimally in the decision because their absolute risk is low.

68
00:09:27,328 --> 00:09:45,828
For example, a systematic review by Guise and coworkers (2010) concludes that the uterine rupture risk was significantly elevated in women undergoing TOLAC—absolute risk of 0.47 percent and relative risk of 20.7—compared with women choosing ERCD.

69
00:09:45,828 --> 00:09:53,466
The Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units (MFMU) Network conducted a prospective study at 19 medical centers .

70
00:09:53,466 --> 00:09:54,991
Shown

71
00:09:55,156 --> 00:10:05,381
in Table 313 are outcomes of nearly 18,000 women attempting TOLAC that were compared with those of more than 15,000 gravidas undergoing ERCD.

72
00:10:05,331 --> 00:10:13,843
The absolute risk of uterine rupture was 0.7 percent compared with no reported uterine ruptures in the ERCD cohort.

73
00:10:13,843 --> 00:10:24,706
Various reports suggest either no or an increased mortality risk for ERCD compared with attempted TOLAC (Guise, 2010; Wen, 2004).

74
00:10:24,706 --> 00:10:27,293
Table 31-3.

75
00:10:27,293 --> 00:10:38,906
Complications in Women with a Prior Cesarean Delivery Enrolled in the Study by the NICHD MFMU, 1999–2002 Maternal morbidity estimates also are conflicting.

76
00:10:38,906 --> 00:10:47,231
The review by Guise and coworkers (2010) observed no significant differences in hysterectomy or transfusion risk.

77
00:10:47,231 --> 00:10:58,393
But, another metaanalysis reported that women undergoing TOLAC were approximately half as likely to require transfusion or hysterectomy compared with those undergoing ERCD .

78
00:10:58,393 --> 00:11:07,393
Conversely, in the MFMU Network study, the risks for transfusion and infection were significantly greater for women attempting TOLAC .

79
00:11:07,393 --> 00:11:13,806
A recent large retrospective Canadian cohort study compared maternal and neonatal outcomes .

80
00:11:13,806 --> 00:11:18,706
The absolute rates of severe maternal morbidity and mortality were low.

81
00:11:18,706 --> 00:11:37,381
However, women attempting TOLAC had significantly higher adjusted risk ratios of uterine rupture (6.41), severe postpartum hemorrhage with transfusion (2.80), and composite maternal morbidity (1.96) compared with women who underwent ERCD.

82
00:11:37,381 --> 00:11:41,106
This disparity is also found among other studies.

83
00:11:41,106 --> 00:11:49,356
Importantly, compared with a successful TOLAC, the risk of these major complications was fivefold greater with a failed TOLAC .

84
00:11:49,356 --> 00:11:51,993
# Perinatal Risks.

85
00:11:51,993 --> 00:12:08,831
In the Canadian study cited previously, rates of composite severe neonatal morbidity and mortality were higher among women delivering after an attempted VBAC compared with those delivering by ERCD—adjusted odds ratio (aOR) 1.49 .

86
00:12:08,831 --> 00:12:17,293
In this study failed VBAC was also associated with a significantly increased rate of neonatal death—aOR 3.22.

87
00:12:17,293 --> 00:12:30,206
Both the prospective MFMU Network study shown in Table 31-3 and review by Guise (2010) found significantly higher perinatal mortality rates with TOLAC compared with ERCD.

88
00:12:30,206 --> 00:12:39,943
In the latter review, the perinatal mortality rate with TOLAC was 0.13 percent compared with 0.05 percent for ERCD.

89
00:12:39,943 --> 00:12:59,168
In the Canadian study cited previously, adjusted risk ratios for neonatal death (3.22) and for composite severe neonatal morbidity and mortality (1.49) were significantly higher among women delivering after an attempted TOLAC compared with those delivering by ERCD .

90
00:12:59,168 --> 00:13:11,981
In another study of nearly 25,000 women with a prior cesarean delivery, the TOLACrelated perinatal death rate was 1.3 per 1000 among 15,515 women .

91
00:13:11,981 --> 00:13:20,281
Although this absolute risk is small, it is 11 times greater than the perinatal risk found in 9014 women with ERCD.

92
00:13:20,281 --> 00:13:29,556
TOLAC also appears to be associated with a higher risk of hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy (HIE) compared with that for ERCD.

93
00:13:29,556 --> 00:13:41,043
The MFMU Network study reported the incidence of encephalopathy at term to be 46 per 100,000 TOLACs compared with zero cases in women undergoing ERCD .

94
00:13:41,043 --> 00:13:53,531
In the review by Guise (2010), the absolute risk of transient tachypnea of the newborn was slightly higher with ERCD compared with TOLAC—4.2 versus 3.6 percent.

95
00:13:53,531 --> 00:14:05,256
But, neonatal bag and mask ventilation was used more often in newborns delivered following TOLAC than in those delivered by ERCD—5.4 versus 2.5 percent.

96
00:14:05,256 --> 00:14:15,156
The 5-minute Apgar scores or neonatal intensive care unit admission rates for newborns delivered by TOLAC did not differ from those delivered by ERCD.

97
00:14:15,156 --> 00:14:18,493
Trial Of Labor Selection Considerations.

98
00:14:18,493 --> 00:14:26,368
Both patients and providers ideally would like individualized risks when counseling regarding the chance of successful TOLAC.

99
00:14:26,368 --> 00:14:31,768
However, few highquality data guide selection of suitable TOLAC candidates.

100
00:14:31,768 --> 00:14:41,381
Several TOLAC/VBAC calculators can be used at entry to prenatal care and again at the time of labor to assist in successful TOLAC prediction.

101
00:14:41,381 --> 00:14:48,156
However, these have not been validated as predictors of adverse maternal outcomes, such as uterine rupture.

102
00:14:48,156 --> 00:14:55,356
Similarly, algorithms and nomograms to aid prediction fail to demonstrated reasonable prognostic value

103
00:14:55,516 --> 00:15:04,378
Grobman, 2007b, 2008, 2021; Metz, 2013; Wyckoff, 2020).

104
00:15:04,328 --> 00:15:12,616
Lipschuetz and colleagues (2020) applied newer statistical analysis techniques to help improve prognostic accuracy.

105
00:15:12,616 --> 00:15:20,803
Despite the limitations of these predictive tools, several points are pertinent to candidate evaluation and are described subsequently.

106
00:15:20,803 --> 00:15:37,278
Current recommendations of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d) are that most women with one prior low transverse hysterotomy are candidates, and if appropriate, they should be counseled regarding both TOLAC and ERCD options.

107
00:15:37,278 --> 00:15:42,128
It is further recommended that home birth is a contraindication for TOLAC.

108
00:15:42,128 --> 00:15:45,041
# Prior Uterine Incision.

109
00:15:45,041 --> 00:15:52,328
Prior Incision Type The type and number of prior cesarean deliveries are overriding factors in recommending TOLAC .

110
00:15:52,328 --> 00:15:56,691
And these caveats aim to avoid intrapartum uterine rupture.

111
00:15:56,691 --> 00:16:05,903
As shown in Table 31-4, women with one prior low transverse hysterotomy have the lowest risk of symptomatic scar separation.

112
00:16:05,903 --> 00:16:11,628
However, asymptomatic dehiscence or complete rupture is still a concern in these women .

113
00:16:11,628 --> 00:16:18,403
The uterine rupture risk in women with a prior vertical incision that did not extend into the fundus is unclear.

114
00:16:18,403 --> 00:16:31,166
Martin (1997) and Shipp (1999) and their associates reported that these low vertical uterine incisions do not have a higher risk for rupture compared with low transverse incisions.

115
00:16:31,166 --> 00:16:45,803
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2015) concluded that although evidence is limited, women with a prior vertical incision in the lower uterine segment without fundal extension may be candidates for TOLAC.

116
00:16:45,803 --> 00:16:54,041
This is in contrast to prior classical or T-shaped uterine incisions, which are considered by most as contraindications to labor.

117
00:16:54,041 --> 00:16:56,653
Table 31-4.

118
00:16:56,653 --> 00:17:03,453
Types of Prior Uterine Incisions and Estimated Risks for Uterine Rupture Figure 31-3.

119
00:17:03,453 --> 00:17:09,616
Extensive “uterine window” in a woman at term with one prior low transverse cesarean delivery.

120
00:17:09,616 --> 00:17:17,403
A. Transabdominal sonogram shows the uterine defect (arrow), bulging amnionic sac, and adjacent anatomy.

121
00:17:17,403 --> 00:17:19,428
B = Bladder.

122
00:17:19,428 --> 00:17:29,016
B. At the time of cesarean delivery, the amnionic sac is seen protruding through the defect and is visible beneath the thin vesicocervical peritoneum.

123
00:17:29,016 --> 00:17:32,953
Reproduced with permission from Dr. Angela Seasley.

124
00:17:32,953 --> 00:17:38,041
The highest risks are with prior vertical incisions extending into the fundus.

125
00:17:38,041 --> 00:17:45,903
Importantly, women with a classical uterine scar may rupture before labor onset, and this may even occur before term.

126
00:17:45,903 --> 00:17:53,628
Chauhun and colleagues (2002) reviewed 157 women with a prior classical cesarean incision.

127
00:17:53,628 --> 00:18:00,228
They used a policy of fetal lung maturity assessment at 36 weeks and then delivery if mature.

128
00:18:00,228 --> 00:18:14,866
With a mean gestational age of 35.6 weeks at delivery, they reported one uterine rupture at 29 weeks that resulted in fetal death; 9percent uterine dehiscence rate; and 25-percent risk of maternal morbidity.

129
00:18:14,866 --> 00:18:25,128
For a woman with a prior classical cesarean delivery, the authors concluded that uterine rupture and dehiscence are neither predictable nor preventable despite early delivery.

130
00:18:25,128 --> 00:18:35,416
The risk for earlier rupture is even greater in women who have undergone open maternal-fetal surgery (Chmait, 2019; Goodnight, 2019).

131
00:18:35,416 --> 00:18:47,441
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2021) recommends delivery between 360/7 and 370/7 weeks for a prior classical incision.

132
00:18:47,441 --> 00:19:00,191
Although few indications dictate a primary classical incision, 53 percent of women undergoing cesarean delivery between 240/7 weeks and 256/7 weeks have such an incision .

133
00:19:00,191 --> 00:19:08,166
By 28 weeks’ gestation, the risk drops to 35 percent and declines to <10 percent by 32 weeks.

134
00:19:08,166 --> 00:19:14,391
The likelihood of classical uterine incision is also increased by noncephalic presentations.

135
00:19:14,391 --> 00:19:25,341
In those instances—for example, preterm breech fetus with an undeveloped lower segment—the “low vertical” incision almost invariably extends into the active segment.

136
00:19:25,341 --> 00:19:35,791
From their review, Moramarco and colleagues (2020) reported that prior preterm classical cesarean delivery was associated with a 1-percent risk for rupture.

137
00:19:35,791 --> 00:19:46,353
Lannon and coworkers (2015) compared 456 women with a prior periviable cesarean delivery with more than 10,000 women whose prior

138
00:19:46,516 --> 00:19:49,491
cesarean delivery was done at term.

139
00:19:49,441 --> 00:19:57,428
The uterine rupture rate was 1.8 percent in the prior periviable group and 0.4 percent in the prior term group.

140
00:19:57,428 --> 00:20:05,403
Of the uterine ruptures in the periviable group, half were in women whose prior uterine incision was described as low transverse.

141
00:20:05,403 --> 00:20:10,603
Harper and associates (2009) did not confirm these findings.

142
00:20:10,603 --> 00:20:14,778
The type of uterine incision cannot be confirmed in all women.

143
00:20:14,778 --> 00:20:28,491
Unless clinical circumstances raise concern for a prior classical uterine incision, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d) consider these women as potential TOLAC candidates.

144
00:20:28,491 --> 00:20:35,028
Special consideration is given to women with uterine malformations who have undergone cesarean delivery.

145
00:20:35,028 --> 00:20:45,278
Earlier reports suggested that the uterine rupture risk in a subsequent pregnancy was greater than the risk in those with a normal uterus and a prior low transverse hysterotomy .

146
00:20:45,278 --> 00:20:52,778
However, in a study of 103 women with müllerian duct anomalies, there were no cases of uterine rupture .

147
00:20:52,778 --> 00:21:02,828
Prior Incision Closure As discussed in Chapter 30 (p. 558), the low transverse uterine incision can be sutured in either one or two layers.

148
00:21:02,828 --> 00:21:10,428
One metaanalysis compared singleversus double-layer closure and locking versus nonlocking suture for uterine closure .

149
00:21:10,428 --> 00:21:16,003
The uterine dehiscence or rupture rates for these closures did not differ significantly.

150
00:21:16,003 --> 00:21:26,316
Both single-layer closure and closure that locked the first layer, however, were associated with a reduced myometrial thickness during subsequent sonographic measurement.

151
00:21:26,316 --> 00:21:39,516
In contrast, Bennich and coworkers (2016) reported that a double-layer closure did not increase the residual myometrial thickness when saline contrast sonography was done several months postpartum.

152
00:21:39,516 --> 00:21:46,966
Our practice at Parkland Hospital is to routinely suture the lower-segment incision with one running, locking suture line.

153
00:21:46,966 --> 00:22:04,178
Number of Prior Cesarean Incisions At least three studies report a doubling or tripling of the uterine rupture rate in women with two compared with one prior transverse hysterotomy (Macones, 2005a; Miller, 1994; Tahseen, 2010).

154
00:22:04,178 --> 00:22:10,241
In contrast, an analysis of the MFMU Network database did not confirm this .

155
00:22:10,241 --> 00:22:26,503
Namely, in 975 women with multiple prior cesarean deliveries, the rupture rate was 0.9 percent and not significantly different from the 0.7-percent rate in 16,915 women with a single prior operation.

156
00:22:26,503 --> 00:22:34,041
Imaging of Prior Incision Sonographic measurement of a prior hysterotomy incision has been used to predict rupture risk.

157
00:22:34,041 --> 00:22:38,816
Large defects in a nonpregnant uterus forecast a greater risk .

158
00:22:38,816 --> 00:22:50,453
Naji and coworkers (2013a,b) found that the residual myometrial thickness decreased as pregnancy progressed and that rupture risk correlated positively with a thinner scar.

159
00:22:50,453 --> 00:23:01,578
Jastrow and colleagues (2010a) did a systematic review of women with a prior low transverse hysterotomy incision who underwent third-trimester sonographic evaluation.

160
00:23:01,578 --> 00:23:07,691
They concluded that a uterine scar defect was strongly predicted by a thin lower uterine segment.

161
00:23:07,691 --> 00:23:14,328
They defined this segment as the smallest measurement between urine in the maternal bladder and amnionic fluid.

162
00:23:14,328 --> 00:23:19,928
That said, they could not find an ideal threshold value to recommend safe TOLAC.

163
00:23:19,928 --> 00:23:34,853
This same group subsequently recruited 1856 women contemplating vaginal birth after a single low transverse incision, and they sonographically measured lower uterine segment thickness between 340/7 and 386/7 weeks .

164
00:23:34,853 --> 00:23:51,716
Women were grouped into three risk categories for uterine rupture during TOLAC based on measurements: high risk <2.0 mm; intermediate risk 2.0 to 2.4 mm; and low risk ≥2.5 mm.

165
00:23:51,716 --> 00:23:58,578
The TOLAC rates were 9, 42, and 61 percent in the three categories, respectively.

166
00:23:58,578 --> 00:24:03,903
Of the 984 TOLACs, there were no symptomatic uterine ruptures.

167
00:24:03,903 --> 00:24:10,866
At Parkland Hospital, we do not assess the lower uterine segment in women with a prior cesarean delivery.

168
00:24:10,866 --> 00:24:13,728
# Prior Uterine Rupture.

169
00:24:13,728 --> 00:24:19,291
Women who have previously sustained a uterine rupture are at greater risk for recurrence.

170
00:24:19,291 --> 00:24:31,816
As shown in Table 31-4, those with a prior lower-segment rupture have up to a 6-percent recurrence risk, whereas prior upper-segment rupture confers a 9- to 32-percent risk

171
00:24:31,972 --> 00:24:36,947
Reyes-Ceja, 1969; Ritchie, 1971).

172
00:24:36,897 --> 00:24:46,059
Sheth (1968) described outcomes of 21 subsequent pregnancies in 13 women who underwent uterine rupture repair.

173
00:24:46,059 --> 00:24:50,809
Rupture recurred in four pregnancies —approximately 20 percent.

174
00:24:50,809 --> 00:24:55,872
Usta and colleagues (2007) reported similar results.

175
00:24:55,872 --> 00:25:04,659
Fox and associates (2014) reported 14 women with prior uterine rupture and 30 women with prior uterine dehiscence.

176
00:25:04,659 --> 00:25:16,684
In 60 subsequent pregnancies, they noted no uterine ruptures or severe complications if women underwent ERCD prior to labor onset or immediately at the onset of preterm labor.

177
00:25:16,684 --> 00:25:22,734
Delivery is recommended between 360/7 and 370/7 weeks’ gestation .

178
00:25:22,734 --> 00:25:25,522
# Interdelivery Interval.

179
00:25:25,522 --> 00:25:35,597
Magnetic resonance imaging studies of myometrial healing suggest that complete uterine involution and restoration of anatomy may require at least 6 months .

180
00:25:35,597 --> 00:25:46,984
As a potential risk for uterine rupture, the relationship between interdelivery interval and uterine rupture in 2409 women with one prior cesarean delivery was examined .

181
00:25:46,984 --> 00:25:51,847
There were 29 women with a uterine rupture—1.4 percent.

182
00:25:51,847 --> 00:26:03,809
Interdelivery intervals ≤18 months were associated with a threefold greater risk of symptomatic rupture during a subsequent TOLAC compared with intervals >18 months.

183
00:26:03,809 --> 00:26:16,584
Similarly, Stamilio and associates (2007) noted a threefold augmented risk of uterine rupture in women with an interpregnancy interval <6 months compared with one ≥6 months.

184
00:26:16,584 --> 00:26:19,534
# Prior Vaginal Delivery.

185
00:26:19,534 --> 00:26:33,547
Prior vaginal delivery, either before or after a cesarean birth, significantly improves the prognosis for a successful TOLAC (Grinstead, 2004; Hendler, 2004; Mercer, 2008).

186
00:26:33,547 --> 00:26:46,822
Prior vaginal delivery also lowers the TOLAC-related risk of subsequent uterine rupture and other morbidities (Cahill, 2006; Hochler, 2014; Zelop, 1999).

187
00:26:46,822 --> 00:26:50,609
# Prior Cesarean Delivery Indication.

188
00:26:50,609 --> 00:26:59,522
Women with a nonrecurring indication—for example, breech presentation— have the highest successful TOLAC rate—nearly 90 percent .

189
00:26:59,522 --> 00:27:16,172
Those with a prior cesarean delivery for fetal compromise have an approximately 80-percent VBAC rate, and for those done for labor arrest, VBAC rates approximate only 60 percent (Bujold, 2001; Peaceman, 2006).

190
00:27:16,172 --> 00:27:23,434
Prior second-stage cesarean delivery can be associated with secondstage uterine rupture in a subsequent pregnancy .

191
00:27:23,434 --> 00:27:34,784
A secondary analysis of the MFMU Cesarean Registry revealed that a prolonged second stage of labor—exceeding 3 hours—during TOLAC will often lead to VBAC.

192
00:27:34,784 --> 00:27:43,259
However, adverse maternal outcomes including uterine rupture or dehiscence were observed to be more common as the second stage lengthens .

193
00:27:43,259 --> 00:27:46,034
# Fetal Size and Lie.

194
00:27:46,034 --> 00:27:51,997
Most studies show that increasing fetal size is inversely related to VBAC rates.

195
00:27:51,997 --> 00:27:55,822
The risk for uterine rupture is less robustly linked.

196
00:27:55,822 --> 00:28:09,297
Zelop and colleagues (2001) studied outcomes of almost 2750 women undergoing TOLAC, and the uterine rupture rate increased—albeit not significantly— with rising fetal weight.

197
00:28:09,297 --> 00:28:21,409
The rate was 1.0 percent for fetal weight <4000 g, 1.6 percent for >4000 g, and 2.4 percent for >4250 g.

198
00:28:21,409 --> 00:28:29,072
Similarly, others have found associated increased risks for uterine rupture with fetuses >4000 g .

199
00:28:29,072 --> 00:28:42,597
With preterm fetuses, compared with term ones, women who attempt a TOLAC have similar or higher VBAC rates and lower uterine rupture rates (Durnwald, 2006; Quiñones, 2005).

200
00:28:42,597 --> 00:28:47,622
Few studies address the choice of TOLAC for a singleton term breech fetus.

201
00:28:47,622 --> 00:28:56,934
Very limited data suggest a possible link with adverse perinatal outcome (Azria, 2012; Macharey, 2017).

202
00:28:56,934 --> 00:29:10,147
Data supporting external cephalic version for breech presentation with a prior cesarean scar also are limited and are derived from small studies (Impey, 2018; Weill, 2017).

203
00:29:10,147 --> 00:29:17,522
From these, version success and adverse event rates appear comparable with women without a prior cesarean delivery.

204
00:29:17,522 --> 00:29:27,984
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020) does not consider a prior uterine incision to be a contraindication for attempted version.

205
00:29:27,984 --> 00:29:33,997
At Parkland Hospital, we do not attempt version or vaginal breech delivery in these women.

206
00:29:33,997 --> 00:29:36,909
# Multifetal Gestation.

207
00:29:36,909 --> 00:29:41,497
Twin pregnancy does not appear to increase the risk of uterine rupture.

208
00:29:41,497 --> 00:29:44,322
In one study of 1850

209
00:29:44,476 --> 00:29:51,651
women with twins, the VBAC rate was 45 percent, and the rupture rate was 0.9 percent .

210
00:29:51,601 --> 00:30:05,626
Similar studies by Cahill (2005) and Varner (2007) and their coworkers reported rupture rates of 0.7 to 1.1 percent and VBAC rates of 75 to 85 percent.

211
00:30:05,626 --> 00:30:16,963
According to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d), women with twins and a prior low transverse hysterotomy can safely undergo TOLAC.

212
00:30:16,963 --> 00:30:19,551
# Maternal Obesity.

213
00:30:19,551 --> 00:30:32,238
Multiple studies have reported an inverse relationship between prepregnancy body mass index (BMI) and successful TOLAC rates (Juhasz, 2005; Wu, 2019).

214
00:30:32,238 --> 00:30:50,463
Hibbard and associates (2006) reported the following rates: 85 percent with a normal BMI, 78 percent with a BMI between 25 and 30, 70 percent with a BMI between 30 and 40, and 61 percent with a BMI ≥40.

215
00:30:50,463 --> 00:30:52,751
# Fetal Death.

216
00:30:52,751 --> 00:30:59,813
Most women with a prior cesarean delivery and fetal death in the current pregnancy would prefer a vaginal delivery.

217
00:30:59,813 --> 00:31:06,163
Although fetal concerns are obviated, available data suggest that maternal risks are increased.

218
00:31:06,163 --> 00:31:18,501
Nearly 46,000 women with a prior cesarean delivery in the MFMU Network database had a total of 209 fetal deaths at an average gestational age of 32.8 weeks .

219
00:31:18,501 --> 00:31:24,901
Of the 158 women who elected TOLAC, the VBAC rate was 87 percent.

220
00:31:24,901 --> 00:31:36,488
In the entire TOLAC group, the uterine rupture rate was 2.4 percent, and in 116 women who underwent an induction of labor, the rupture rate was 3.4 percent.

221
00:31:36,488 --> 00:31:41,876
Labor induction for second-trimester fetal demise has a low complication rate .

222
00:31:41,876 --> 00:31:44,888
Labor And Delivery Considerations.

223
00:31:44,888 --> 00:31:46,913
# Timing.

224
00:31:46,913 --> 00:31:59,063
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a) recommends delaying nonmedically indicated deliveries until 39 completed weeks of gestation or beyond.

225
00:31:59,063 --> 00:32:14,313
Data from Tita and colleagues in Figure 31-4 show significant and appreciable adverse neonatal morbidity has been reported with ERCD before 39 completed weeks (Chiossi, 2013; Clark, 2009).

226
00:32:14,313 --> 00:32:19,451
Thus, if ERCD is planned, it is essential that the fetus be mature.

227
00:32:19,451 --> 00:32:26,251
Conversely, delaying delivery beyond 40 weeks is associated with higher neonatal morbidity rates.

228
00:32:26,251 --> 00:32:28,913
Figure 31-4.

229
00:32:28,913 --> 00:32:36,113
Neonatal morbidity rates seen with 13,258 elective repeat cesarean deliveries.

230
00:32:36,113 --> 00:32:39,076
Any adverse outcome includes death.

231
00:32:39,076 --> 00:32:42,263
Sepsis includes suspected and proven.

232
00:32:42,263 --> 00:32:48,976
RDS = respiratory distress syndrome; TTN = transient tachypnea of the newborn.

233
00:32:48,976 --> 00:32:53,463
Accurate pregnancy dating is paramount prior to ERCD.

234
00:32:53,463 --> 00:33:06,026
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019c) offers guidelines to assist in accurate pregnancy dating using a combination of last menstrual period and sonography.

235
00:33:06,026 --> 00:33:13,663
The most accurate method to confirm or establish gestational age is by sonographic measurement of an early embryo-fetus.

236
00:33:13,663 --> 00:33:16,176
# Intrapartum Care.

237
00:33:16,176 --> 00:33:37,076
Because uterine rupture during TOLAC may be unpredictable, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d) recommends that labor in women attempting TOLAC should be undertaken in facilities that can provide emergency cesarean delivery for situations associated with immediate threats to the life of the women or her fetus.

238
00:33:37,076 --> 00:33:46,926
If those resources are not available, the College recommends discussion of the hospital’s resources and availability of providers with the patient considering TOLAC.

239
00:33:46,926 --> 00:33:53,801
Ultimately, the best alternative may be referral to an institution with resources appropriate for TOLAC.

240
00:33:53,801 --> 00:33:58,588
Some argue that these provisions deny women full access to choices.

241
00:33:58,588 --> 00:34:11,113
For example, in an earlier survey of Ohio hospitals, 15 percent of Level I, 63 percent of Level II, and 100 percent of Level III institutions met these requirements .

242
00:34:11,113 --> 00:34:33,301
Moreover, an obstetrical anesthesia workforce survey reported that due to staffing limitations, TOLAC was allowed in only 88 percent of hospitals with ≥1500 annual deliveries, in 59 percent of those with 500 to 1499 deliveries, and in 43 percent of those with <500 deliveries .

243
00:34:33,301 --> 00:34:37,126
# Cervical Ripening and Labor Stimulation.

244
00:34:37,126 --> 00:34:43,338
Labor induction is associated with a higher TOLAC failure rate compared with spontaneous labor.

245
00:34:43,338 --> 00:34:55,713
The risks for uterine rupture, however, are less clear with induction or augmentation, with the exception of prostaglandin E1 (PGE1)—misoprostol—which is contraindicated

246
00:34:55,876 --> 00:35:07,638
Of other considerations, induction or augmentation is ideally avoided in women with an unknown prior incision type, an unfavorable cervix, or pregnancy >40 weeks.

247
00:35:07,588 --> 00:35:14,126
At Parkland Hospital we do not induce or augment labor pharmacologically in women electing TOLAC.

248
00:35:14,126 --> 00:35:18,263
Instead, we attempt induction only by amniotomy.

249
00:35:18,263 --> 00:35:27,763
Oxytocin Use of oxytocin for labor induction or augmentation has been implicated in increased uterine rupture rates in women undergoing TOLAC .

250
00:35:27,763 --> 00:35:43,163
In the MFMU Network study reported by Landon (2004), uterine rupture was more frequent in women induced with oxytocin alone—1.1 percent— compared with those presenting in spontaneous labor—0.4 percent.

251
00:35:43,163 --> 00:35:48,738
Labor augmentation was associated with uterine rupture in 0.9 percent.

252
00:35:48,738 --> 00:36:01,513
Among women in this cohort without a prior vaginal delivery, the uterine rupture risk associated with oxytocin induction was 1.8 percent—a fourfold greater risk compared with spontaneous labor .

253
00:36:01,513 --> 00:36:08,163
In contrast, in one case-control study, induction was not associated with a higher rupture risk .

254
00:36:08,163 --> 00:36:16,876
Cahill (2008) and Goetzl (2001) and their coworkers reported a dose-related risk of rupture with oxytocin.

255
00:36:16,876 --> 00:36:27,576
Prostaglandins Various prostaglandin preparations commonly employed for cervical ripening or labor induction are discussed in Chapter 26 (p. 489).

256
00:36:27,576 --> 00:36:36,626
Wing and colleagues (1998) compared misoprostol versus oxytocin for labor induction in women with a prior cesarean delivery.

257
00:36:36,626 --> 00:36:43,788
They terminated their trial after two of the first 17 women assigned to misoprostol developed a uterine rupture.

258
00:36:43,788 --> 00:36:46,388
Other studies confirmed this.

259
00:36:46,388 --> 00:37:01,151
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d) recommends that misoprostol should not be used for cervical ripening or labor induction in women at term with a prior cesarean delivery or major uterine surgery.

260
00:37:01,151 --> 00:37:06,601
Studies using other prostaglandin agents for labor induction are contradictory.

261
00:37:06,601 --> 00:37:17,963
Ravasia and coworkers (2000) compared uterine rupture rates in 172 women given PGE2 gel with 1544 women in spontaneous labor.

262
00:37:17,963 --> 00:37:28,213
The rupture rate of 2.9 percent was significantly greater in women treated with PGE2 gel compared with 0.9 percent in those with spontaneous labor.

263
00:37:28,213 --> 00:37:33,376
LydonRochelle and associates (2001) found similar results.

264
00:37:33,376 --> 00:37:44,551
In the MFMU Network study cited previously, however, the uterine rupture rate was 1.4 percent when any prostaglandin was used in combination with oxytocin .

265
00:37:44,551 --> 00:37:52,538
But, in the subgroup of 227 women in whom labor was induced with a prostaglandin alone, there were no ruptures.

266
00:37:52,538 --> 00:38:00,501
Similar findings were reported with intravaginal prostaglandins, which were not associated with a greater uterine rupture risk .

267
00:38:00,501 --> 00:38:14,663
These latter investigators, along with Kayani and colleagues (2005), found that sequential use of a prostaglandin followed by oxytocin was associated with a threefold greater rupture risk compared with spontaneous labor.

268
00:38:14,663 --> 00:38:38,113
Mechanical Methods In a retrospective study of 2479 women with a prior cesarean delivery, the uterine rupture risk using a transcervical Foley catheter for labor induction (1.6 percent) was not significantly greater than that with spontaneous labor (1.1 percent)—or with amniotomy with or without oxytocin (1.2 percent) .

269
00:38:38,113 --> 00:38:46,426
In 101 women induced with a balloon catheter, a 50-percent successful TOLAC rate and no uterine ruptures was reported .

270
00:38:46,426 --> 00:39:00,526
In contrast, Hoffman and coworkers (2004) described 138 women who underwent preinduction cervical ripening with a Foley catheter compared with 536 women who entered labor spontaneously.

271
00:39:00,526 --> 00:39:11,638
The inordinately high intrapartum uterine rupture rate of 6.5 percent following Foley catheter cervical ripening was greater than the 1.9-percent rate with spontaneous labor.

272
00:39:11,638 --> 00:39:14,551
# Epidural Analgesia.

273
00:39:14,551 --> 00:39:21,401
Concerns that epidural analgesia for labor might mask the pain of uterine rupture have not been verified.

274
00:39:21,401 --> 00:39:30,688
Fewer than 10 percent of women with scar separation experience pain and bleeding, and fetal heart rate decelerations are the most likely sign of rupture .

275
00:39:30,688 --> 00:39:41,676
That said, Cahill and associates (2010a) documented that more frequent episodes of epidural catheter dosing were associated with increasing uterine rupture rates.

276
00:39:41,676 --> 00:39:45,001
VBAC rates are similar, and in

277
00:39:45,172 --> 00:39:53,009
some cases higher, among women with labor epidural analgesia compared with those using other analgesia forms .

278
00:39:52,959 --> 00:39:59,522
Perhaps related, almost a fourth of VBAC deliveries were completed with either forceps or vacuum .

279
00:39:59,522 --> 00:40:12,084
The American Academy of Pediatrics and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2017) have concluded that epidural analgesia may safely be used during TOLAC.

280
00:40:12,084 --> 00:40:15,234
# Uterine Scar Exploration.

281
00:40:15,234 --> 00:40:26,772
Following VBAC, some clinicians routinely document the integrity of a prior scar by placing a hand through the dilated cervix and along the inner surface of the lower uterine segment.

282
00:40:26,772 --> 00:40:32,284
But, routine uterine exploration is considered by others to be unnecessary.

283
00:40:32,284 --> 00:40:45,034
In a longitudinal study of 3469 women who had a VBAC, seven uterine dehiscences and one uterine rupture yielded an overall event rate of 0.23 percent .

284
00:40:45,034 --> 00:40:52,897
These investigators concluded that transcervical evaluation need only be performed for those with severe third-stage bleeding.

285
00:40:52,897 --> 00:41:04,897
Currently, the benefits of routine scar evaluation in the asymptomatic woman are unclear, however, surgical correction of a dehiscence is necessary for associated significant bleeding.

286
00:41:04,897 --> 00:41:12,884
At Parkland Hospital, we routinely examine the lower uterine segment following a VBAC and document its integrity.

287
00:41:12,884 --> 00:41:21,472
A large tear, a breach into the peritoneal cavity, and presence of active bleeding favor a decision for laparotomy and repair.

288
00:41:21,472 --> 00:41:23,697
Uterine Scar Rupture.

289
00:41:23,697 --> 00:41:26,097
# Diagnosis.

290
00:41:26,097 --> 00:41:37,684
Progress of labor in women attempting TOLAC is similar to normal labor, and no specific pattern presages uterine rupture (Graseck, 2012; Harper, 2012b).

291
00:41:37,684 --> 00:41:47,434
Before hypovolemic shock develops, symptoms and physical findings in women with uterine rupture may appear bizarre unless the possibility is kept in mind.

292
00:41:47,434 --> 00:41:55,559
For example, hemoperitoneum from a ruptured uterus may result in diaphragmatic irritation with pain referred to the chest.

293
00:41:55,559 --> 00:42:02,147
This may suggest a diagnosis of pulmonary or amnionic fluid embolism instead of uterine rupture.

294
00:42:02,147 --> 00:42:15,709
As the example shown in Figure 31-5, the most common sign of uterine rupture is a nonreassuring fetal heart rate pattern with variable decelerations that may evolve into late decelerations and bradycardia.

295
00:42:15,709 --> 00:42:27,359
In one report of 36 cases during TOLAC, fetal signs of uterine rupture were evident in 24, maternal signs in eight, and a combination of maternal and fetal in three .

296
00:42:27,359 --> 00:42:37,597
Few women experience cessation of contractions following uterine rupture, and the use of intrauterine pressure catheters does not assist reliably in the diagnosis .

297
00:42:37,597 --> 00:42:40,284
Figure 31-5.

298
00:42:40,284 --> 00:42:45,459
Fetal heart rate tracing in a woman whose uterus ruptured during labor while pushing.

299
00:42:45,459 --> 00:42:53,097
The rupture apparently stimulated a reflex push, after which uterine tone diminished and fetal bradycardia worsened.

300
00:42:53,097 --> 00:43:02,072
The clinical appearance of uterine rupture associated with a TOLAC may mirror that of placental abruption but with little appreciable pain or tenderness.

301
00:43:02,072 --> 00:43:12,072
Also, because most women in labor are treated for discomfort with either narcotics or epidural analgesia, pain and tenderness may not be readily apparent.

302
00:43:12,072 --> 00:43:21,884
Ultimately, the diagnosis of uterine rupture becomes evident because of fetal distress and occasionally because of maternal hypovolemia from concealed hemorrhage.

303
00:43:21,884 --> 00:43:29,784
If the fetal presenting part has already entered the pelvis with labor, loss of station may be detected by pelvic examination.

304
00:43:29,784 --> 00:43:42,797
If the fetus is partly or totally extruded from the uterine rupture site, abdominal palpation or vaginal examination may be helpful to identify the presenting part, which will have moved away from the pelvic inlet.

305
00:43:42,797 --> 00:43:47,547
A firm contracted uterus may at times be felt alongside the fetus.

306
00:43:47,547 --> 00:43:51,409
Emergent sonography in the labor unit may be helpful.

307
00:43:51,409 --> 00:43:53,634
# Management.

308
00:43:53,634 --> 00:44:01,184
With rupture and expulsion of the fetus into the peritoneal cavity, the chances for intact fetal survival are poor.

309
00:44:01,184 --> 00:44:09,109
Fetal condition depends on the degree to which the placental implantation remains intact, although this can change within minutes.

310
00:44:09,109 --> 00:44:18,747
The only chance of intact fetal survival is afforded by immediate delivery—most often by laparotomy—otherwise, hypoxia is inevitable.

311
00:44:18,747 --> 00:44:23,397
If rupture is followed by total placental separation, very few

312
00:44:23,548 --> 00:44:27,210
neurologically intact fetuses will be salvaged.

313
00:44:27,160 --> 00:44:32,598
Thus, even in the best of circumstances, some fetal outcomes will be impaired.

314
00:44:32,598 --> 00:44:36,035
The Utah experiences are instructive here .

315
00:44:36,035 --> 00:44:47,310
Of the 35 laboring patients with uterine rupture, the decision-to-delivery time was <18 minutes in 17, and none of these infants had an adverse neurological outcome.

316
00:44:47,310 --> 00:44:58,348
Of the 18 born >18 minutes from decision time, the three infants with long-term neurological impairments were delivered at 31, 40, and 42 minutes.

317
00:44:58,348 --> 00:45:07,210
There were no deaths, but severe neonatal neurological morbidity developed in 8 percent of this group of 35 women with uterine rupture.

318
00:45:07,210 --> 00:45:15,235
Importantly, delivery within 30 minutes did not prevent every case of low cord pH or low 5-minute Apgar score.

319
00:45:15,235 --> 00:45:23,473
When uterine rupture was identified and delivery was accomplished is less than 30 minutes, there was no longterm neonatal morbidity.

320
00:45:23,473 --> 00:45:36,023
In the MFMU Network study cited earlier, seven of the 114 uterine ruptures associated with TOLAC—6 percent—were complicated by development of neonatal encephalopathy .

321
00:45:36,023 --> 00:45:40,510
Neonatal mortality rate is likewise increased several fold.

322
00:45:40,510 --> 00:45:48,023
In a study using the Swedish Birth Registry, the risk of neonatal death following uterine rupture was 5 percent .

323
00:45:48,023 --> 00:45:51,698
Maternal deaths from uterine rupture are uncommon.

324
00:45:51,698 --> 00:46:06,323
Of 2.5 million women who gave birth in Canada between 1991 and 2001, there were 1898 cases of uterine rupture, and four of these—0.2 percent—resulted in maternal death .

325
00:46:06,323 --> 00:46:11,735
In other regions of the world, however, maternal mortality rates are much higher.

326
00:46:11,735 --> 00:46:18,185
From rural India, the maternal mortality rate associated with uterine rupture was 30 percent .

327
00:46:18,185 --> 00:46:23,273
Following complete rupture during TOLAC, hysterectomy may be required.

328
00:46:23,273 --> 00:46:29,498
In selected cases, however, suture repair with uterine preservation may be performed.

329
00:46:29,498 --> 00:46:35,860
Subsequent pregnancy following such repair was described earlier (p. 576).

330
00:46:35,860 --> 00:46:44,423
Bladder injury may be comorbid, and interrogation of its integrity is prudent (Phipps, 2005; Webb, 2000).

331
00:46:44,423 --> 00:46:47,523
Multiple Repeat Cesarean Deliveries.

332
00:46:47,523 --> 00:46:57,448
The incidences of some common complications for women with a prior transverse cesarean delivery who undergo an ERCD were shown in Table 31-3 .

333
00:46:57,448 --> 00:47:04,935
Rates of these complications and of other serious maternal morbidity rise with the number of prior cesarean deliveries .

334
00:47:04,935 --> 00:47:16,498
First, the incidence of placenta accreta spectrum (PAS), which includes placenta accreta, increta, or percreta increases markedly with the number of previous hysterotomies.

335
00:47:16,498 --> 00:47:32,135
In an MFMU Network cohort of 30,132 women who had from one to six repeat cesarean deliveries, a significant association between an accruing number of cesarean deliveries and PAS in women with placenta previa was noted .

336
00:47:32,135 --> 00:47:45,860
The PAS rate grew from 11 percent for women with a placenta previa undergoing their second cesarean delivery to 67 percent for women undergoing their fifth (Fig. 43-12, p. 761).

337
00:47:45,860 --> 00:47:54,323
Indeed, almost half of cesarean hysterectomies done at Parkland Hospital are in women with one or more prior cesarean deliveries .

338
00:47:54,323 --> 00:47:57,060
Figure 31-6.

339
00:47:57,060 --> 00:48:04,798
Maternal-Fetal Medicine Units Network: rates of some complications with increasing number of repeat cesarean deliveries.

340
00:48:04,798 --> 00:48:12,285
Other maternal morbidity data come from the same MFMU Network cohort and are shown in Figure 31-6 .

341
00:48:12,285 --> 00:48:25,748
In addition, rates of bowel or bladder injury, admission to an intensive care unit or need for ventilator therapy, maternal mortality, and length of surgery and hospitalization showed significantly rising trends.

342
00:48:25,748 --> 00:48:33,073
Similar results have been reported by others (Nisenblat, 2006; Usta, 2005).

343
00:48:33,073 --> 00:48:47,873
More difficult to quantify are risks for bowel obstruction and pelvic pain from peritoneal adhesive disease, both of which increase after each successive cesarean delivery (Andolf, 2010; Mankuta, 2013).

344
00:48:47,873 --> 00:48:56,773
From the United Kingdom Obstetric Surveillance System (UKOSS), adverse sequelae in women with five or more cesarean deliveries were described .

345
00:48:56,773 --> 00:49:03,248
These women had significantly higher rates of morbidity compared with those with one prior operation.

346
00:49:03,248 --> 00:49:15,798
Specifically, the major hemorrhage rate increased 18-fold; visceral damage, 17-fold; critical care admissions, 15-fold; and delivery <37 weeks, sixfold.

347
00:49:15,798 --> 00:49:17,435
Much of

348
00:49:17,596 --> 00:49:22,996
this morbidity was in the 18 percent who had a placenta previa or PAS.

349
00:49:22,946 --> 00:49:29,683
This same study showed higher neonatal complication rates stemming mainly from indicated premature delivery.

350
00:49:29,683 --> 00:49:33,646
Vaginal Birth After Cesarean—2021.

351
00:49:33,646 --> 00:49:43,746
Unfortunately for women and their providers, no large randomized trials compare maternal or neonatal outcomes in those pursuing either TOLAC or ERCD.

352
00:49:43,746 --> 00:49:49,583
Most studies have compared actual routes of delivery rather than the intended route of delivery.

353
00:49:49,583 --> 00:49:55,208
Thus, we agree with Scott (2011) regarding a common-sense approach.

354
00:49:55,208 --> 00:50:03,358
The woman—and if she wishes, her partner—are encouraged to actively participate with the provider in an informed-consent discussion.

355
00:50:03,358 --> 00:50:07,508
Patient decision aids often assist shared decision making .

356
00:50:07,508 --> 00:50:19,596
Although the absolute rates of adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes are low, TOLAC is associated with a higher rate of both maternal and neonatal morbidity and mortality compared with ERCD.

357
00:50:19,596 --> 00:50:30,296
Counseling should include documentation of the prior uterine incision and discussion of risks, benefits, and probable outcomes associated with TOLAC or ERCD.

358
00:50:30,296 --> 00:50:35,058
This includes consideration of risks involving future pregnancies.

359
00:50:35,058 --> 00:50:43,483
Ideally, counseling begins preconceptionally and continues throughout pregnancy, with flexible options extending up to delivery.

360
00:50:43,483 --> 00:50:56,146
For women who desire TOLAC despite a factor that increases their specific risk, additions to the consent form are recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019d).

361
00:50:56,146 --> 00:51:01,283
Bonanno and colleagues (2011) have provided such an example.

362
00:51:01,283 --> 00:51:10,971
Tsakiridis and coworkers (2018) have compared national guidelines of societies in the United States, Canada, and the United Kingdom.

363
00:51:10,971 --> 00:51:17,933
A composite of these recommendations of professional society guidelines is shown in Table 31-5 .

364
00:51:17,933 --> 00:51:23,433
Guidelines that tend to be more conservative are shown in Table 31-6.

365
